1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Running one Claude call without blocking

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
is only half the story.

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
The moment you need 50 results,

5
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,000
you're managing concurrency, streams,

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
and the limits that push back when you go too fast.

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson walks through fanning out requests,

8
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
consuming a stream, and staying under your rate limit.

9
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's start with the payoff,

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
because it's the whole reason we bother with concurrency.

11
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Say you have 50 independent Claude calls to make,

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
none of them depending on another.

13
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Run them one by one,

14
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
and your total time is the sum of all 50.

15
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Run them concurrently, and something better happens.

16
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,000
You start every request,

17
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
so they're all in flight at the same time.

18
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Then you await them as a group,

19
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
and gather the results together.

20
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Now your total time trends toward your single slowest call,

21
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
not the sum of all of them.

22
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
That overlap, all that waiting happening at once,

23
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
instead of back to back, is exactly what you're buying.

24
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The catch is doing it without overwhelming the API,

25
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
which is the rest of this lesson.

26
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Three tools do most of the work when you fan out,

27
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
so let's define them. First, gather.

28
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
That's a call that takes many core routines,

29
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,000
starts them all together,

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
and waits until everyone has finished,

31
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
handing you back the whole set of results.

32
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Second, the semaphore.

33
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
It's just a counter with a cap.

34
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
It limits how many requests are allowed to run at once,

35
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
and any request beyond the cap

36
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
waits its turn until a slot opens.

37
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Third, bounded parallelism,

38
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,000
which is really the goal the first two serve.

39
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
You run concurrently, but only so many at a time,

40
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
say five or 10, instead of launching everything at once.

41
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Gather, semaphore, bounded parallelism.

42
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Together they let you go fast without flooding the API.

43
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Here's the mistake almost everyone makes first,

44
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
and how to fix it.

45
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
The naive approach is unbounded fan out.

46
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
You just launch all 50 or all 5,000 requests at once.

47
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
It's the simplest code you could write,

48
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
and for a handful of calls it's totally fine,

49
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
but push it, and you instantly blow past your rate limit.

50
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The API starts rejecting requests with 429 errors,

51
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
and now you're handling a wave of failures

52
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
you created yourself.

53
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
The fix is bounded fan out.

54
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
You wrap your calls in a semaphore

55
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
that keeps only, say, 10 in flight at any moment.

56
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
It's a little more code,

57
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
but it's dramatically more reliable under real load.

58
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
The rule of thumb is simple.

59
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Never fan out unbounded against a rate-limited API.

60
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Streaming is the other async pattern you'll use constantly,

61
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
and it works a little differently.

62
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,000
When you set streaming on,

63
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
you don't wait for the whole response.

64
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Instead, the call gives you an async iterator,

65
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
and you loop over it with async four.

66
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Each time through, you get a small event.

67
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The text of the answer arrives as deltas, tiny fragments,

68
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
and your job is to append them in order as they come.

69
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
You keep accumulating those pieces

70
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
until the content block signals that it's complete.

71
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The reason this matters is user experience.

72
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Instead of staring at a spinner for several seconds,

73
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
the person sees words appear almost immediately

74
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
and keep flowing while the rest of the answer

75
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
is still being generated.

76
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Same total time, but it feels far faster.

77
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Now, let's talk about what pushes back

78
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
when you go too fast.

79
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
The API enforces rate limits,

80
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
caps on requests, and tokens per minute.

81
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Cross one and you get a 429, too many requests.

82
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Back pressure is the principle for respecting that.

83
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
You let the slowest stage in your pipeline set the pace,

84
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
so you're never submitting new work

85
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
faster than results are draining out.

86
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The practical tool is the semaphore we mentioned.

87
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Capping in-flight requests to a fixed number

88
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
and holding new work until a slot frees up.

89
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
And when a 429 does slip through,

90
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
you don't just retry instantly and make it worse.

91
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
You wait and you back off exponentially

92
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,000
before trying again.

93
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Rate limits, back pressure, a semaphore, and back off.

94
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
That's the toolkit for staying inside the lines.

95
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Let's go deeper on retries,

96
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
because getting this wrong wastes real money and time.

97
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
At scale, transient failures are normal,

98
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
and you already know the transient versus permanent rule

99
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
from the architecture lessons.

100
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
What's new here is how you schedule

101
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
the retries under concurrency.

102
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
You use exponential back off,

103
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
waiting a little after the first failure,

104
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
longer after the second, longer still after the third.

105
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
On top of that, you add random jitter to each wait,

106
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
so that a whole batch of failed requests

107
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
doesn't retry in perfect lockstep

108
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and stampede the API again.

109
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
And the old rule bites harder here.

110
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Only retry the errors that can clear,

111
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
because a permanent failure retried

112
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,000
across 50 parallel branches burns budget 50 times faster.

113
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:01,000
It's worth drawing a hard line here,

114
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
because this is the most common mix-up.

115
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Async concurrency and the batch's API aren't the same tool.

116
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Async concurrency means firing off

117
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
many real-time calls at once.

118
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Everyone runs at full price,

119
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
and they all count against your rate limit,

120
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
but you get every answer back within seconds.

121
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,000
That's what you reach for

122
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
when a user or a downstream system is waiting.

123
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
The batch's API is the opposite trade.

124
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
You submit one asynchronous job, it runs at half price,

125
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
and most batches finish within the hour,

126
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
with 24 hours as the ceiling.

127
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,000
That's for bulk work, when nobody is waiting on any result.

128
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
So the real question is simply whether someone is waiting.

129
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Answer that, and the choice makes itself.

130
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
One last piece makes concurrent code,

131
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
production grade, and it's error isolation.

132
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
When you fan out 50 calls,

133
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
you have to decide what happens if one of them fails.

134
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
The wrong answer is letting a single exception

135
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
tear down the whole group,

136
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
throwing away 49 good results because one went bad.

137
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
The better pattern is to gather results

138
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
in a way that captures each branch's outcome independently,

139
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
whether it returned a result or raised an exception.

140
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Then, after the whole group comes back,

141
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
you walk through the outcomes,

142
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,000
keep the successes, and handle or retry just the failures.

143
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
That way, one flaky call, one timeout, one content filter

144
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
doesn't sink an otherwise successful batch.

145
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Designed for the fact that at scale,

146
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
some branch always fails, and make sure it fails alone.

147
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Let's frame the big picture.

148
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Concurrency gives you a handful of tools

149
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
for running many clogged calls safely.

150
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Gather, semaphores, streaming, and backoff,

151
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
each with its own job.

152
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
The name matters less than the discipline behind them.

153
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Keep concurrency bounded and isolate failures.

154
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
One thing to watch, many real-time calls

155
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
is not the same as the batch's API.

156
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Just keep that in mind.

157
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
That wraps up concurrency, streaming, and backpressure.

158
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,000
I'll see you in the next one.

